AB  - This study is located in the area of university work-integrated learni
ng (WIL) management, in particular the implementation of both placemen
t and non-placement WIL. The focus of the study is on enhancing WIL co
ordinators’ understanding of WIL as a system, using activity theory as
 a framework, and to mobilize this understanding to address challenges
 in practice. To this end WIL coordinators at two South African univer
sities attended WIL challenge workshops in which an activity system-de
rived diagnostic toolkit was introduced. The verbal discussions and di
agrams presented by the coordinators were analyzed for evidence of sys
tematic understanding of WIL, challenges and future possibilities. Fin
dings from the study revealed that WIL coordinators successfully used 
the toolkit to identify system-level constraints and were inspired to 
engage in further investigations. The authors thus argue that the diag
nostic toolkit could be used with WIL coordinators at other universiti
es as a useful approach to understanding and optimizing their WIL prac
tices. 
