AB  - This study explores the integration of self-regulated learning (SRL) s
trategies in management education to cultivate entrepreneurial mindset
s among business students. Drawing on self-regulated learning theory a
nd reflective practice, we analyze the reflective diaries of three coh
orts of students enrolled in a one-year master's in Business Administr
ation program at a Swedish university. Our findings identify two disti
nctive learners; guided learners, who thrive in structured and collabo
rative environments, and autonomous learners, who excel in independent
 and self-directed settings. The study highlights the dynamic transiti
ons between these learner types, influenced by feedback internalizatio
n and task-complexity. By emphasizing the importance of reflective dia
ries, this research underscores the role of SRL in fostering entrepren
eurial skills, enhancing student engagement, and promoting lifelong le
arning. The implications for business schools include the need for tai
lored educational approaches that support diverse learning preferences
 and the gradual development of students’ autonomy. Ultimately, this s
tudy contributes to the broader discourse on entrepreneurial education
 by demonstrating how SRL can empower students to navigate the complex
ities of the global business landscape with confidence and creativity.
 
